<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Parakeet's privacy manifest (R8-22). It declares the "required reason" APIs the shipped app imports, and only those.
	scripts/check_privacy_manifest.sh compares it with the first-party sources (App/ and ChirpKit/), with the vendored
	Needle and llama.cpp runtimes and, in CI, with every binary of the built app, so a new use or a removed one fails
	until this file says so. Packages that ship a manifest of their own (GRDB does, in its resource bundle, and Xcode
	merges it) need nothing here. Code that ships none (FluidAudio, WhisperKit, needle-c, llama.cpp) is covered by what
	the linked binary imports. Not checked: Apple frameworks loaded at run time, which are Apple's own.
	Not used (checked in the sources and in every binary): system boot time (systemUptime, mach_absolute_time) and
	active keyboards. clock_gettime, which the Rust and llama.cpp code imports, is not on Apple's list.
	NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty: the app has no account and no server of its own. Text the person sends to a
	cloud model or voice they chose goes to that provider; what that means for the App Store privacy answers is the
	owner's call when an App Store Connect record exists (docs/distribution.md).
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- UserDefaults, CA92.1 (information only the app itself reads): the app's own settings stores, on the standard
		defaults, never an app group (ChirpFeatures: SettingsStore, SpeechRouteStore, LanguageModelProviderStore,
		CompanionSettingsStore, JevSettingsStore, VoiceSettings, StructuredResultGate, CreateChoices, CreateRecipe). -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- Disk space, E174.1 (check there is room before writing files): a model download refuses when the volume
		lacks room (LlamaCppModelAssets) and a meeting refuses to start when the iPhone is almost full
		(MeetingCoordinator). 85F4.1 (show it to the person): the meeting screen shows how many minutes of audio fit. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>E174.1</string>
				<string>85F4.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- File timestamp, C617.1 (metadata of files inside the app container). Not first-party code: the Swift sources
		read file sizes only (attributesOfItem [.size], size and free-space resource keys), never a date, and nothing
		shows a file date to the person. The stat, fstat, lstat and fstatat imports come from the Rust standard library
		inside two statically linked libraries that ship no manifest. needle-c (Needle 3, scripts/build_needle.sh): the
		app calls only needle_v3_load, which reads the model, <Application Support>/Models/needle3/needle3.cact, with
		std::fs::read (fstat); at the pinned needle-rs (4de50494) every file access outside its tests reads a model,
		weights or vocabulary file at a path the caller passes. FluidAudio's NeMo text-processing library: its C API takes
		text, never a path (nemo_normalize and the rest of nemo_text_processing.h), so the app cannot point it at a file. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
